<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simon Thassi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 135 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simon Thassi was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mattathias">Mattathias</a> and the last of the Maccabee brothers, who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> from 142 BCE. He expelled the Seleucid garrison from the Akra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and in 140 BCE an assembly confirmed him as high priest, commander and ethnarch, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a>. He was murdered by his son-in-law Ptolemy in 135 BCE and succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simon-thassi.jpg" alt="Simon Thassi" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Reverdy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simon_Thassi_medal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Simon Thassi was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mattathias">Mattathias</a> and one of the Maccabean brothers who led the revolt against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> with Judas Maccabaeus and Jonathan Apphus. His epithet Thassi suggests &quot;the Wise&quot;, and it can also mean &quot;the Director&quot;, &quot;the Guide&quot;, &quot;the Man of Counsel&quot; and &quot;the Zealous&quot;. He is also called Simon the Hasmonean, Simon Maccabee or Simon Maccabeus.</p><p>The Seleucid king Antiochus VI appointed him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a>, or military commander, of the coast from the Ladder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In that post he took Beth-zur and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Joppa</a>, garrisoned them with Jewish troops and built the fortress of Adida.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the Seleucid general Diodotus Tryphon captured Jonathan, the people assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> chose Simon as their leader. He completed the fortification of the city and secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Joppa</a>, and at Hadid he blocked Tryphon's advance. Tryphon demanded a ransom and Jonathan's sons as hostages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> and 1 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a> both say Simon agreed, although he knew he would be deceived, so that the people could see he had done all he could. Jonathan was killed anyway and the hostages were not returned.</p><p>Simon then sided with the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-ii-nicator">Demetrius II</a> and asked for freedom from taxation, which was granted. This implied recognition of Judea's political independence. A large assembly of priests, people and elders decreed that he should be leader and high priest &quot;forever, until there should arise a faithful prophet&quot;. He became High Priest and Ethnarch, the first prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasmonean-dynasty">Hasmonean dynasty</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> recognised the new dynasty about 139 BCE, when his delegation was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Simon and two of his sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mattathias">Mattathias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, were killed at a banquet at Dok by his son-in-law Ptolemy, the Seleucid governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a>. His third son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a> succeeded him as high priest and ruler, but at first could not capture Ptolemy, who held John's mother hostage.</p><p>The name Simon, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> form Simeon, became the most popular male name for some three centuries in the Hasmonean Kingdom and Roman Judaea, both to honour the leader who had won independence and because it did not sound strange to Greek ears.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simon Thassi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By 140 BCE his brother <b>Simon</b> had won full independence and was confirmed as high priest and ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasmonean-kingdom">Hasmonean Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.86,35.46&amp;year=-145" aria-label="Jericho on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5463 1004 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Simon Thassi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5913 1274h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5913 1274h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.86,35.46&amp;year=-145">What was here in 145 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
